---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StepsStrip from '../components/StepsStrip.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'One size for every photo?',
    a: 'Yes. Set the size once and every photo on the tray is resized to it: a width, a height, or both. With both numbers set, each photo fits inside that frame and keeps its own shape, so a mixed folder of wide and tall photos all come out inside the same frame.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the format change too?',
    a: 'No. Each photo keeps its own format: a JPG stays a JPG, a PNG stays a PNG. If you want the folder in a different format instead, the batch convert page is next door; if you want an exact KB target, the batch compress page.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if a photo is smaller than the size I set?',
    a: 'It is enlarged to the size, and its result line says the dimensions it reached. Bigger is not sharper: no detail is added, here or anywhere.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a per-day allowance?',
    a: 'No. The metered converters ration this job by minutes and operations; this page has no counter to hit, because the work runs on your device and costs this site nothing per photo.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where do my photos go?',
    a: 'Nowhere. They are read into this page and resized in your browser. Watch the network panel while it works if you want to check.',
  },
];
const faqJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
});
const howToJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: "How to resize many images to one size",
  step: [
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 1, name: "Pick your images", text: "Drop images on the tray, or click to pick them. JPEG, PNG and WebP all read in. They are never uploaded." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 2, name: "Set the one size", text: "Type a width, a height, or both. Both numbers mean every photo fits inside that frame, keeping its own shape; the quality setting applies to JPG and WebP results." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 3, name: "Run the batch", text: "Press 'Resize the batch'. Each photo is decoded and redrawn at the size, one at a time, with its own result line, and keeps its own format." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 4, name: "Download the results", text: "Download photos one by one, or take the whole batch away as a single ZIP." },
  ],
});
---

<Base title="Batch resize images: a folder to one pixel size, free · Benchzy" description="Resize a folder of photos to one pixel size in a single run, inside your browser: a width, a height, or a frame both ways, every photo keeping its own format. No upload, no account, no operation counter.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4">
    <section class="mt-8">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-accent-deep">Image tools · batch</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-black mt-2">Batch resize images</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        A whole folder, one size out. Set a width, a height, or both, and every photo is
        resized in a single run, each keeping its own format and its own shape inside the
        frame. One photo at a time is the
        <a class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-deep" href="/resize-image/">resize image page</a>;
        if instead the folder has to fit one exact KB target, the
        <a class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-deep" href="/batch-compress-images/">batch compress page</a> is next door.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="ticket mt-6 p-5 sm:p-7" id="bi-tool" data-mode="resize">
      <div id="bi-tray" class="tray p-6 text-center cursor-pointer" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Pick images">
        <p class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Drop images on the tray, or click to pick them</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-soft mt-2">JPEG · PNG · WebP · tested with 50 files in one run · they stay on this device</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-soft mt-1">JPG, PNG, WebP and iPhone HEIC photos work here.</p>
        <input id="bi-file" type="file" accept="image/*" multiple class="hidden" />
      </div>
      <p class="file-loaded hidden" data-loaded-for="bi-file" role="status"></p>

      <ul id="bi-list" class="mt-6"></ul>

      <div class="grid gap-6 mt-6 md:grid-cols-[1fr_320px]">
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">One size for the batch</h2>
          <div class="flex items-end gap-3 mt-3 flex-wrap">
            <span>
              <label class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft block" for="bi-width">Width (px)</label>
              <input id="bi-width" type="number" min="1" max="20000" step="1" placeholder="e.g. 1200" class="mt-1 border border-ink bg-ticket px-3 py-2 font-mono w-32" />
            </span>
            <span class="font-mono text-ink-soft pb-2">x</span>
            <span>
              <label class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft block" for="bi-height">Height (px)</label>
              <input id="bi-height" type="number" min="1" max="20000" step="1" placeholder="optional" class="mt-1 border border-ink bg-ticket px-3 py-2 font-mono w-32" />
            </span>
            <span class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <label class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft" for="bi-quality">Quality (JPG/WebP)</label>
              <input id="bi-quality" type="number" min="1" max="100" step="1" value="90" class="border border-ink bg-ticket px-3 py-2 font-mono w-24" />
            </span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-ink-soft text-sm leading-relaxed mt-3 max-w-md">
            One number is enough: a width alone sets every photo to that width, shapes kept.
            Both numbers mean every photo fits inside that frame. Each photo keeps its own
            format, and every result line prints the dimensions before and after.
          </p>
          <h3 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft mt-5">Presets, the same sizes as the single-photo page</h3>
          <div id="bi-presets" class="flex gap-2 mt-3 flex-wrap">
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-w="413" data-h="531" title="35 x 45 mm at 300 DPI">Passport photo · 413 x 531</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-w="600" data-h="600" title="2 x 2 in at 300 DPI">US passport · 600 x 600</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-w="1080" data-h="1080" title="Instagram and feeds">Square post · 1080 x 1080</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-w="1080" data-h="1350" title="the 4:5 frame">Portrait post · 1080 x 1350</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-w="1080" data-h="1920" title="the 9:16 frame">Story · 1080 x 1920</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-w="1280" data-h="720" title="YouTube thumbnail size">Video thumbnail · 1280 x 720</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-w="1920" data-h="1080" title="the 1080p frame">Full HD · 1920 x 1080</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-w="1600" data-h="1600" title="the 1600 px long side listing sites ask for, shape kept">eBay / Marketplace · long side 1600</button>
          </div>
          <p class="text-ink-soft text-sm leading-relaxed mt-2 max-w-md">
            A preset fills the two boxes above; every photo then fits inside that frame with
            its shape kept.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">File spec</h2>
          <dl id="bi-spec" class="mt-2">
            <div class="spec-row"><dt>files on the tray</dt><dd>0</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <button id="bi-run" class="btn-primary w-full mt-5" type="button" disabled>Resize the batch</button>
          <p id="bi-status" class="font-mono text-sm mt-4 leading-relaxed" aria-live="polite">Pick images to begin.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="bi-result" class="receipt hidden mt-8">
        <div class="tear pt-5 flex items-center gap-4 flex-wrap">
          <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">Results</h2>
          <span class="stamp" id="bi-stamp">Done on your device</span>
        </div>
        <div id="bi-rows" class="mt-4 grid gap-1"></div>
        <a id="bi-zip" class="btn-primary inline-block no-underline mt-4 hidden" href="#" download>Download all as one ZIP</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <StepsStrip who="For a folder that must come out at one size: listing photos for a shop, a shoot for a website, thumbnails for a catalogue." steps={[{ n: '01', t: 'Pick your images' }, { n: '02', t: 'Set the one size' }, { n: '03', t: 'Run and download the results' }]} />

    <section class="mt-12 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">How it works</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          Each photo is decoded by the open-source jSquash codecs, redrawn at the size you
          set, and written back in its own format, one file at a time. It is the same engine
          as the single-photo resize page, run over the whole tray. The first run loads the
          image program (about 2 MB, one time only); the download is how we protect your
          privacy, because the work then happens on your device and your photos never leave
          it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Honest limits</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          Resizing cannot add detail: a photo enlarged to the frame stays as soft as it was,
          and the result line prints the dimensions it reached either way. Hidden camera and
          location data (EXIF) does not survive re-encoding, which most people want, but if
          you need it kept, keep the originals. The batch was tested with 50 files in one
          run; past that, the limit is your device's memory, not a rule of ours.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 mb-4">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4">
        {faq.map((f) => (
          <article class="ticket-soft p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold">{f.q}</h3>
            <p class="text-ink-soft leading-relaxed mt-1">{f.a}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqJson}></script>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={howToJson}></script>
  <script>
    import '../scripts/batch-image.js';
  </script>
</Base>
